{
    "attributes": {
        "action": "Publish clearer criteria for UK Emergency Alert System usage",
        "additional_details": "We think this would maintain trust for genuine emergencies. The Emergency Alerts system was designed to warn the public about imminent, life-threatening emergencies requiring urgent action, such as wildfires, severe flooding, and extreme storms. In our view, on 14 August 2026, an alert was sent nationwide as a general wildfire awareness reminder rather than for a specific, actionable emergency. We believe this risks undermining public trust, encouraging users to disable alerts on their phones. Without published clear criteria, future misuse may render this vital mechanism ineffective.",
        "background": "Following the 14 August 2026 nationwide wildfire alert, which we believe was used for general information rather than a specific life-threatening emergency, we call on the UK Government to publish clearer criteria for when the Emergency Alerts system may be deployed.",
